Actually, let me look at this from a different angle. Let me check the `moffset` function and the `mget` function for the `FILE_STRING` type handling. Let me also look at the `file_softmagic` flow more carefully.

Actually, let me look at the `mcopy` function again for the `FILE_STRING` case. When `indir == 0` and type is `FILE_STRING` or `FILE_PSTRING`, it falls through to the default case which does a `memcpy`. Let me check:

```c
if (type == FILE_OFFSET) {
        (void)memset(p, '\0', sizeof(*p));
        p->q = offset;
        return 0;
}

if (offset >= nbytes) {
        (void)memset(p, '\0', sizeof(*p));
        return 0;
}
if (nbytes - offset < sizeof(*p))
        nbytes = nbytes - offset;
else
        nbytes = sizeof(*p);

(void)memcpy(p, s + offset, nbytes);
```

This copies up to `sizeof(*p)` bytes from `s + offset` to `p`. If `offset < nbytes`, this is fine. But `sizeof(*p)` is the size of the union, which is `MAXstring = 128` bytes. So it copies up to 128 bytes from the input buffer. If the input buffer is smaller than 128 bytes at that offset, it copies fewer bytes. This seems fine.

Let me look at the `moffset` function which computes the next offset for continuation matching.

